WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.560
YURI GRINSHTEYN: In
Stackdriver, metrics

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.685
are used to understand the
change of numeric values

00:00:03.685 --> 00:00:04.410
over time.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.510
And logs, they're used
to capture specific point

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:07.237
in time events.

00:00:07.237 --> 00:00:08.820
But what if you need
to understand how

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.990
those events change over time?

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:13.560
You can use Stackdriver to
create metrics from logs,

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:14.820
and I'll show you how.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.190
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:17.927
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.320
Google Cloud logs contain
a wealth of information,

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.790
but it's hard to analyze trends
without additional steps,

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.340
such as exporting
them to BigQuery

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.290
and using a visualization tool.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:34.720
Stackdriver log-base metrics
allow you to count records

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:35.740
based on a filter.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.360
You can also track
numeric values

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.130
to easily convert logs
to metrics you can

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:40.690
use for analysis and alerting.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:42.530
Let's start with
a simple example.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:44.830
We have an application that
writes errors out to logs.

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:46.705
Now we'd like to create
a chart that shows us

00:00:46.705 --> 00:00:48.790
how many errors we get
and an alert that lets

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:50.620
us know if we get too many.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.260
We can create a counter metric
from those error logs simply

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.060
by clicking the
Create metric button

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.080
and selecting counter
as the metric type.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.080
The metric will count the
number of entries that

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.120
match our filter every minute.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.400
The metric is then available
in Stackdriver Monitoring

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.590
under the logging
slash user domain.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:07.860
We can create charts
using in dashboards

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.790
and configure alerting
policies using it.

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:11.240
That was easy enough.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.390
We now have a way to use
log-base metrics to count

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:15.050
how many errors we see.

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:17.320
Let's take a look at a
more complicated scenario.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:19.120
In the case of a
successful transaction,

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.370
our application writes out
a log message that tells us

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:23.200
how long the transaction took.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.590
We want to track that value
and chart application latency.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:29.170
Here's the log message
with the latency recorded.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:31.630
To do that, we'll create
a distribution metric.

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:34.450
To do that, click on Create
metric, select Distribution

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.150
as the metric type, and
specify a regular expression

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:38.710
to extract the
numeric value we're

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:41.120
after from a field
in the log payload.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:42.970
The counter metric we
created earlier just

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.460
counted the number of entries
that matched our filter.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:47.630
This distribution
metric will instead

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:49.930
track the numeric
value we care about.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:51.610
Just like before,
our new metric is

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.350
available in
Stackdriver monitoring,

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:56.020
and we can create a chart
tracking its values over time.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:57.520
We can also use our
log-base metrics

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.380
for alerting, just
like any other metric

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.030
available in
Stackdriver monitoring,

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:03.402
by using it as a condition
and then alerting policy.

00:02:03.402 --> 00:02:05.860
One more great thing you should
know about log-base metrics

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:07.300
is that they make it
really easy to see

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.850
the log entries that were used
to build the metric values.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:12.130
From a chart, you can
simply click on View Logs

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:13.630
to see the logs for the metric.

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:16.172
While logs and metrics are often
used for different purposes,

00:02:16.172 --> 00:02:17.713
you may have a need
to create metrics

00:02:17.713 --> 00:02:19.230
that help you analyze log data.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:22.480
Stackdriver log-base metrics,
both counter and distribution,

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:23.850
allow you to do just that.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.350
Thank you for joining me today,
and come back soon for more.

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:28.510
This is the Stack Doctor,
stay healthy out there.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

